reviews.text_detailed_comments:
(1) In Intro the paper claim that CCRC is more practical, as a high CCRC indicates better alignment between correctness and confidence; I believe this also applied to ECE. If so, what is the advantage of CCRC over ECE?
(2) The computation of URE needs a pre-defined correctness constraint, this limits the flexibility of model evaluation; in fact, defining a proper correctness constraint may be hard in many applications. It would be good to see what the authors’ thoughts are on this problem;
(3) In Figure 3, we can see that a small change of correctness requirement can lead to dramatic change of UR, e.g. Fig.3-4, a change of 0.01 dice can lead to 0.15 UR change for U-Net. Does this mean URE is very sensitive to the correctness requirement, or in other words, no stable? Because changing the requirement by very little may lead to a different model recommendation;
(4) How the conclusion (URE is stable on unseen samples) is drawn from Table 1? It’s hard to judge whether a ~10% frequency violation is good or bad based on the current content.

The paper propose a novel evaluation metric that quantify the model correctness and confidence in an unified way. The paper is generally well-written. Further discussion and justification on the limitation and experiment evaluation may help improve the paper.

reviews.text_strengths:
Explore the use of Transformer in medical image segmentation;
Utilize the high-level low-resolution output to guide low-level high-resolution prediction;
Considerable segmentation performance improvement compared to prior works.

reviews.text_weaknesses:
The writing of this paper should be improved, especially for the preciseness and conciseness. Many sentences are confusing and sometimes misleading;
The parallel branching design and windowed attention is not novel; the former is similar to [24] in the paper, and the latter is similar to Swin Transformer;
Some major details and justification of the proposed methods are missing;

reviews.text_detailed_comments:
In Intro the paper writes “Transformer-based methods frequently adopt down-sampling…”. In fact, vanilla VIT only adopts single patch projection operation for downsampling, and thus, using “frequently” is not correct here;
Also in Intro, the authors claim that the proposed method “guide self-attention to focus on the hard regions”, this needs further justification; the method in its current form, does not seem to have any mechanism to focus on “hard region”;
How do patch merging and patch expanding work? I cannot find any explanation of this two modules; the paper lack significant details and reference on the transformer branch;
For the L2G mechanism, the proposed methods firstly apply a very fine-grained self-attention in a window-based fashion, then apply a global self-attention. This means that a single L2G block essentially apply self-attention operation twice. Will this require significant computational resources?
Does PP-guided Self-attention generalize to multi-class segmentation? the current implementation heavily relies on the absolute value of the prediction logits, which does not seem to be applicable for multi-class problem；
In the ablation study, please further clarify each setting, e.g. what exactly is the “backbone”? Also, what would be the result if we use simple self-attention instead of PPFormer Block?

This paper propose two new strategy to enhance polyp segmentation, from the perspective of improving local information and enhance the boundary feature representation. However, some major design motivation and details are missing. Also, the writing in the paper are not concise and precise. Thus, the paper needs further improvement.

I could not see any technical novelty in terms of the approaches and methods used even though the methods used are solid and well accepted within the community;
A bit more of explainability exploration could have helped to make the paper stronger, what exactly the model learned.

I think it is important to facilitate data and code sharing and this paper can help to this.

